# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 7 joins two syllabus heads. **Head VIII — Gender-Based Violence** names: defining gender-based violence; theories of violence against women; structural and direct forms of violence; forms of violence against women in Pakistan (honour killing, rape, domestic violence, acid attacks, harassment and others); and laws, policies and strategies to combat it. **Head IX — Case Studies** names three Pakistani women: **Mukhtaran Mai**, **Malala Yousafzai** and **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**. This document teaches every line, and adds the newest one the examiner has used: **masculinity studies and violence** (2026).

In the descriptive paper Topic 7 has had ==10.5 visits==: nine full questions and three short notes. **Forms and strategies** dominate — 2016, 2017 and 2023 asked almost the same question — and **theory** follows (power and control 2020; root causes 2025). The **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016** was named in 2024. Each case study has been asked: Mukhtaran Mai (2019), Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy (2020) and Malala (2025). The topic also supplies **12 of the 140 MCQs** — violence as **learned behaviour** (2023), **1 in 3** women (2024), **socially constructed** violence (2024), the **2016** honour-killing law (2026), Malala's birth year (**1997**), Mukhtaran Mai's region (**South Punjab**).

::: simple
**Gender-based violence** is harm done to a person **because of their gender** — most often by men against women, because society gives men power over women and treats women's bodies, choices and "honour" as men's business. It includes beating, rape, acid attacks, honour killings and harassment, but also threats, control and denying a woman money or freedom. It happens at **home**, at **work**, in the **street**, **online** and even in **custody**.
:::

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic, the scale of the problem and the map of Topic 7.
2. **Part One** has nine chapters on gender-based violence: definitions (1); theories (2); power and control, and structural and direct violence (3); root causes and how they intersect (4); forms and sites in Pakistan (5); honour killing (6); laws, the Punjab Act of 2016 and government initiatives (7); consequences and strategies (8); and masculinities (9).
3. **Part One-B** has the three case studies: Mukhtaran Mai (10), Malala Yousafzai (11) and Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy (12).
4. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
5. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
6. **Part Four** is a bank of about 125 one-liners, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
7. **Part Five** is the revision sheet.

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 7

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 7 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.6,10.4
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Gender-based violence (GBV) ★ | Violence directed at a person **because of their sex or gender**, or that affects one sex disproportionately (MCQ 2025).
Violence against women (VAW) ★ | Any act of gender-based violence that results in physical, sexual or psychological harm to women, including threats and coercion (**DEVAW 1993**).
Domestic violence ★ | Violence by a family member or intimate partner in the home — beating, sexual abuse, threats, control, denial of money.
Intimate partner violence | Violence by a husband, former husband or partner.
Honour killing ★ | Killing of a woman (sometimes a man) by relatives who claim she brought **dishonour** on the family — *karo-kari*, *siyahkari*, *tor tora*.
Femicide | The intentional killing of women and girls because they are women.
Acid attack | Throwing acid to disfigure, usually the face; punished under the **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011**.
Sexual harassment ★ | Unwelcome sexual advances, requests or conduct; at work it is covered by the **2010** Act.
Rape | Sexual intercourse without consent; in the Penal Code since the **2006** Act.
Custom-based violence | *Vani* or *swara* (girls given in settlement), marriage to the Qur'an, forced marriage, denial of inheritance — made crimes by the **2011** Act.
Cyber violence | Online harassment, blackmail, stalking and image-based abuse; covered by **PECA 2016**.
Structural violence ★ | Harm built into social structures — poverty, unequal laws, denial of education — with no single person "hitting" (**Galtung 1969**).
Direct violence ★ | Visible, physical or verbal violence by a person against another — beating, rape, murder.
Cultural violence | Beliefs and symbols that make direct and structural violence seem normal or right (**Galtung 1990**).
Power and control ★ | The idea that abusers use violence to gain and keep **control** over a partner (**Duluth wheel, 1981**).
Cycle of violence | Tension building → explosion → reconciliation ("honeymoon") → calm (**Lenore Walker 1979**).
Coercive control | A pattern of domination through isolation, intimidation, surveillance and degradation (Evan Stark 2007).
Social learning theory ★ | Violence is **learned** by watching and copying others (MCQs 2023, 2024).
Ecological model | Violence explained at four levels — individual, relationship, community, society (**Lori Heise 1998**).
Hegemonic masculinity ★ | The dominant ideal of manhood — tough, controlling, the protector of "honour" (**Connell**).
Protection order | A court order directing an abuser to stay away from, and not contact, a woman (**Punjab Act 2016**, s. 7).
Impunity | Escape from punishment — e.g. honour killers pardoned by their own family under the old law.
:::

## 0.2  The scale of the problem

::: fig bars | Violence against women in Pakistan, 2024 — cases reported (SSDO).
@colour 2F7D4F
Kidnapping and abduction: 24439 *
Rape: 5339
Domestic violence: 2238
Honour killings: 547
:::

::: data The numbers to remember
- **World:** about **1 in 3** women have experienced physical or sexual violence (WHO, ==2021==; MCQ 2024). In ==2023==, about **85,000** women and girls were killed intentionally, **51,100** of them by partners or family members — **one every 10 minutes** (UNODC and UN Women).
- **Pakistan:** **28%** of ever-married women have experienced physical violence since age 15, and **34%** spousal physical, sexual or emotional violence; **56%** of those who suffered violence never sought help (**PDHS 2017–18**).
- **Reported cases, 2024:** **32,617** GBV cases, with conviction rates of only about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing (**SSDO**); at least **405** honour killings (**HRCP**).
:::

::: fig flow | The map of Topic 7.
@per 4
Define: what GBV is (Ch. 1)
Explain: theories, power and control, structural and direct violence, root causes (Ch. 2–4)
Describe: forms and sites in Pakistan; honour killing (Ch. 5–6)
Respond: laws, the Punjab Act 2016, consequences, strategies, masculinities (Ch. 7–9)
Case studies: Mukhtaran Mai, Malala, Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy (Ch. 10–12)
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
Every full question on violence has **two or three parts**: "define… forms… strategies" (2016, 2017, 2023); "what is GBV… forms under the Punjab Act… consequences" (2024); "root causes… how they intersect" (2025). Learn a **five-part frame** for every answer: (1) a precise **definition** (DEVAW 1993); (2) **data** (PDHS, SSDO, HRCP); (3) **theory** (power and control, Galtung, ecological model); (4) **forms, causes or laws** as asked; (5) **strategies** organised as prevention, protection, prosecution, provision and partnership. For the case studies, always give the **background**, the **event**, the **legal and social response** and an **evaluation**.
:::
